{"id": 3591944, "title": "CVE-2026-3805: use after free in SMB connection reuse", "submitted": "2026-03-08", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3591944", "excerpt": "## Summary\n\nA heap-use-after-free occurs in `smb_send_open()` at `lib/smb.c` when curl processes two SMB URLs targeting the same host. The function `smb_parse_url_path()` sets `req->path` as a non-owning pointer into `smbc->share` (connection-owned memory). During connection reuse, the needle connection is freed via `Curl_conn_free()` → `smb_conn_dtor()`, which frees `smbc->share`, but `req->path` (on the easy handle) still references the freed buffer. The subsequent `strlen(req->path)` in `smb_send_open()` reads freed heap memory.\n\n## Affected Version\n\ncurl 8.19.0-DEV (master branch, built March 8 2026)\nPlatform: Ubuntu 22.04 on x86_64 (WSL2)\nBuilt with: gcc, OpenSSL, --enable-smb, -fsanitize=address\n\n## Steps To Reproduce\n\n1. Clone and build curl from master with ASAN and SMB enabled:\n\n    git clone https://github.com/curl/curl.git && cd curl\n    autoreconf -fi\n    ./configure --with-openssl --enable-smb --without-libpsl\n    make CFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address -g -O0 -fno-omit-frame-pointer\" LDFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address\" -j$(nproc)\n\n2. Start the attached fake SMB server (fake_smb_server.py) in one terminal:\n\n    python3 fake_smb_server.py 5445\n\n3. In another terminal, run curl with two SMB URLs to the same host:\n\n    ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=./lib/.libs \\\n    ./src/.libs/curl -u guest:guest \\\n      \"smb://127.0.0.1:5445/share1/file1\" -o /dev/null \\\n      \"smb://127.0.0.1:5445/share2/file2\" -o /dev/null\n\n4. ASAN reports heap-use-after-free in smb_send_open.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nIn `smb_parse_url_path()` (lib/smb.c ~line 398-435):\n- `smbc->share = strdup(path)` allocates \"share2/file2\" (13 bytes)\n- The `/` is replaced with `\\0`, splitting it into \"share2\\0file2\\0\"\n- `req->path = slash` points 7 bytes into `smbc->share`\n\nWhen the second URL reuses the pooled connection from URL #1:\n- `url_find_or_create_conn()` finds a match and frees the needle connection\n- `Curl_conn_free()` → `smb_conn_dtor()` → `free(smbc->share)`\n- `req->path` now dangles into freed heap\n\nThen `smb_request_state()` → `smb_send_open()`:\n```c\nconst size_t byte_count = strlen(req->path) + 1;  // UAF read\n\n## Impact\n\nHeap-use-after-free (CWE-416) triggered by a simple two-URL curl command line. Requires no authentication to a real SMB server  the UAF occurs client-side before any server response to the second request. This results in a guaranteed crash (DoS). If the freed 13-byte he", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.92, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "9a674ee6f7ba", "rev_source": "master on 2026-03-08", "counts": {"grounded": 11, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "A heap-use-after-free occurs in smb_send_open() when processing two SMB URLs targeting the same host, causing a crash or potential memory corruption.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "A use-after-free occurs in smb_send_open() when processing two SMB URLs targeting the same host", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The code shows req->path is set to a pointer inside smbc->share (line 431). When connection reuse occurs, the old connection's smb_conn_dtor frees smbc->share (line 390), but req on the easy handle still holds the dangling pointer. smb_send_open then reads it via strlen (line 750). The code's own comment confirms this exact issue.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 431, "note": "req->path = slash; makes req->path point into smbc->share", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 390, "note": "smb_conn_dtor frees smbc->share via Curl_safefree", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 750, "note": "smb_send_open calls strlen(req->path) reading the dangling pointer", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 379, "note": "Comment in smb_easy_dtor explicitly acknowledges: 'If the connection is destroyed first, req->path points to free'd memory.'", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The flaw is located in lib/smb.c at smb_send_open() and smb_parse_url_path()", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Both functions exist in lib/smb.c at the cited locations and contain the relevant code.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 397, "note": "smb_parse_url_path definition starts here", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 745, "note": "smb_send_open definition starts here", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "Triggered by sending two SMB URLs to the same host, causing connection reuse and freeing of the needle connection", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Connection reuse frees the needle connection via Curl_conn_free, which destroys the meta_hash and runs smb_conn_dtor, freeing smbc->share while req->path still points into it.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/url.c", "line": 556, "note": "Curl_conn_free destroys conn->meta_hash, which invokes smb_conn_dtor", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 390, "note": "smb_conn_dtor frees smbc->share", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "Results in a guaranteed crash (denial of service) and potential memory corruption if the freed heap is reallocated with attacker data", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The UAF read in smb_send_open causes a guaranteed crash (DoS). The freed 13-byte region could be reallocated with attacker-influenced data, potentially leading to information disclosure or memory corruption, though the report does not demonstrate exploitation beyond DoS.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 750, "note": "strlen(req->path) reads freed memory, causing UAF", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "Reproduced by running curl with two SMB URLs to the same host after building with ASAN and SMB enabled", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The reproduction steps are concrete and the code confirms the bug is reachable by the described two-URL command line. The fake SMB server is only needed to accept the connection; the UAF occurs client-side before any server response.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/smb.c", "line": 379, "note": "Code comment confirms the bug exists and is reachable via connection reuse", "valid": true}]}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [], "key_reasons": ["The code contains a use-after-free: req->path points into connection-owned smbc->share, which is freed during connection reuse while req remains on the easy handle.", "The smb_easy_dtor comment explicitly acknowledges this exact issue: 'If the connection is destroyed first, req->path points to free'd memory.'", "The flaw is triggered by a normal, legitimate command line (two SMB URLs to the same host), not a crafted exploit command.", "The UAF causes a guaranteed crash (DoS) and is a heap-use-after-free (CWE-416), which could potentially be exploited further if the freed heap is reallocated with attacker-influenced data.", "The project's 'not security issues' list does not cover client-side memory-safety bugs triggered by normal usage; the 'Tricking a user' section applies to misleading command lines, not legitimate multi-URL transfers, and the 'NULL dereferences and crashes' section addresses server-triggered crashes."], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report. The code confirms the use-after-free: req->path points into smbc->share (lib/smb.c:431), which is freed in smb_conn_dtor (line 390) when the connection is reused, and smb_send_open reads the dangling pointer via strlen (line